\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Mitigation spectrum on ETTh2 forecasting. Methods ordered by representation preservation (CKA, $h{=}96$). LoRA preserves representations (CKA$\approx $0.98) while \emph  {improving} over zero-shot. Mean $\pm $ std; Forgetting\% = (fine-tuned MSE $-$ zero-shot MSE) / zero-shot MSE $\times $ 100. \textbf  {Two caveats on comparability, added in revision.} Forgetting\% divides by the single shared zero-shot printed in each horizon header, not by each run's own zero-shot; the anchoring and LoRA runs measure their own at $0.127$--$0.131$, so their figures here are inflated by $1.2$--$3.4$~pp. And the rows are not matched on training-set size: B and C use $n{=}500$ training samples while L2-SP, EWC and LoRA use $n{=}1000$ (the $n$ column counts seeds, not samples). Matched at $n{=}1000$, $h{=}96$, each against its own zero-shot: full fine-tuning $.936$/$+13.4$, L2-SP $\lambda {=}0.01$ $.939$/$+16.0$, L2-SP $\lambda {=}0.1$ $.965$/$+13.4$, EWC $.972$/$+14.9$, LoRA $.979$/$-9.2$. On that comparison L2-SP $\lambda {=}0.1$ ties full fine-tuning rather than losing to it, so anchoring is not uniformly harmful; the dissociation the table is cited for---CKA not predicting utility---is unaffected.}}{14}{table.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:mitigation_spectrum}{{7}{14}{Mitigation spectrum on ETTh2 forecasting. Methods ordered by representation preservation (CKA, $h{=}96$). LoRA preserves representations (CKA$\approx $0.98) while \emph {improving} over zero-shot. Mean $\pm $ std; Forgetting\% = (fine-tuned MSE $-$ zero-shot MSE) / zero-shot MSE $\times $ 100. \textbf {Two caveats on comparability, added in revision.} Forgetting\% divides by the single shared zero-shot printed in each horizon header, not by each run's own zero-shot; the anchoring and LoRA runs measure their own at $0.127$--$0.131$, so their figures here are inflated by $1.2$--$3.4$~pp. And the rows are not matched on training-set size: B and C use $n{=}500$ training samples while L2-SP, EWC and LoRA use $n{=}1000$ (the $n$ column counts seeds, not samples). Matched at $n{=}1000$, $h{=}96$, each against its own zero-shot: full fine-tuning $.936$/$+13.4$, L2-SP $\lambda {=}0.01$ $.939$/$+16.0$, L2-SP $\lambda {=}0.1$ $.965$/$+13.4$, EWC $.972$/$+14.9$, LoRA $.979$/$-9.2$. On that comparison L2-SP $\lambda {=}0.1$ ties full fine-tuning rather than losing to it, so anchoring is not uniformly harmful; the dissociation the table is cited for---CKA not predicting utility---is unaffected}{table.caption.10}{}}
